WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:09.320
Losing something we love, or someone who is
dear to us, can cause us a lot of pain.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:17.880
To accept the loss and overcome the pain,
we often go through five stages of grief.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:27.040
Denial, anger, bargaining, depression, and
finally acceptance.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.120
First comes Denial.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:35.520
When we experience a loss, we feel shocked
and numb, and then simply deny what happened.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.840
The refusal to accept the truth temporarily
protects us from all the negative thoughts

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.320
and consequences of the tragedy.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.360
Denial gives us a bit of room to breathe.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.200
Then comes Anger.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:56.320
We feel furious and resentful, often blaming
ourselves or others for the catastrophe.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.600
Anger shields us from pain.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:04.840
We feel less vulnerable and more in control
of the situation.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.840
Then there is Bargaining.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.080
It is an attempt to regain control and negotiate
the reality of the loss.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:20.480
We try to make promises or seek outside solutions
to somehow reverse the irreversible.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.040
When the loss sinks in, Depression follows.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.320
We go through feelings of sadness and despair.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.320
We withdraw from others.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.240
As we face reality, depression can help us
process our grief.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.960
Healing can begin.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.680
Lastly, there is acceptance.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.400
We come to terms with what has happened.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:49.160
This does not mean we forget, but we find
a way to integrate the loss into our life.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:54.360
We may now honor the memories of those who
have departed and look ahead, knowing that

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.120
after all, life goes on.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.800
Getting to acceptance, however, does not mean
it is over.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.920
Grief is a complex emotional response to loss,
particularly the loss of someone we love or

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.120
something deeply meaningful — like a job.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:20.520
This process is unique to each of us and is
not always linear.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:27.680
Often we cycle back, revisit stages or experience
some entirely new emotions.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.800
Sometimes it can feel like we are regressing,
but with the support of others and each new

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.240
day, time will help heal our wounds.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.480
Elisabeth Kübler-Ross, a psychiatrist who
supported those facing their own death, observed

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.880
the five stages in the 1960es.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.592
She later wrote:

00:02:49.592 --> 00:02:52.160
"The most beautiful people we have known are

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:58.800
those who have known defeat, known suffering,
known struggle, known loss, and have found

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.280
their way out of the depths.”

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:08.000
What do you think? Have you ever experienced a significant loss
in life?

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.520
And if so, did you go through these 5 stages
or did you take another path to deal with

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:14.440
it?

